WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.250 --> 00:00:05.010
This episode of Real Engineering is brought
to you by Skillshare, home to over 20,000

00:00:05.010 --> 00:00:07.819
classes that could teach you a new life skill.

00:00:07.819 --> 00:00:13.799
As WW2 drew to a close and the race to Berlin
reached it’s finale, two Soviet armies converged

00:00:13.799 --> 00:00:15.900
on the Third Reich capital.

00:00:15.900 --> 00:00:20.300
Marshal Georgy Zhukov from the North, and
Marshal Ivan Konev from the South.

00:00:20.300 --> 00:00:24.850
Motivated to beat the Americans and British
to claim Nazi technology and plunder for themselves.

00:00:24.850 --> 00:00:30.179
The Battle for Berlin raged for 17 days as
the soviet forces surrounded the city, but

00:00:30.179 --> 00:00:34.670
the Nazi’s surrender may have come sooner,
if not for one of the largest defensive structures

00:00:34.670 --> 00:00:35.670
ever built.

00:00:35.670 --> 00:00:42.260
As the 150th and 171st Soviet Rifle Divisions
began advancing across the Spree river, they

00:00:42.260 --> 00:00:45.200
met the Zoo tower, located within the Berlin
Zoo.

00:00:45.200 --> 00:00:49.829
A behemoth structure built as a centre for
anti-aircraft operations.

00:00:49.829 --> 00:00:56.289
Equipped with four 12.8 cm Flak 40 anti-aircraft
guns, together capable of firing 96 rounds

00:00:56.289 --> 00:01:02.060
per minute, and with a group of 20 mm and
37 mm anti-aircraft guns on the lower platforms,

00:01:02.060 --> 00:01:04.750
the Zoo Tower was a veritable death castle.

00:01:04.750 --> 00:01:09.160
A death castle, with no glaring design flaw
to lead to its destruction.

00:01:09.160 --> 00:01:14.030
Any aircraft that dared enter it’s 10 km
range was likely to meet its end, but on this

00:01:14.030 --> 00:01:18.140
day the Zoo tower turned its attention to
the ground and the approaching soviet armies

00:01:18.140 --> 00:01:21.670
and began unleashing powerful and accurate
fire.

00:01:21.670 --> 00:01:26.000
Reducing their tanks to smouldering scraps
of metal in short time, and halting their

00:01:26.000 --> 00:01:27.000
progress.

00:01:27.000 --> 00:01:32.100
The Soviet’s ordered the tower to be shelled
at range from their most powerful 203 mm Howitzer,

00:01:32.100 --> 00:01:37.340
a purpose built bunker buster, but the 100
kg rounds had little effect.

00:01:37.340 --> 00:01:42.439
With 2.5 metre thick steel reinforced concrete
walls, the tower was practically immune to

00:01:42.439 --> 00:01:43.759
shell damage.

00:01:43.759 --> 00:01:46.829
Even if a hole could be punched through with
repeated shots.

00:01:46.829 --> 00:01:51.340
The tower would remain standing, with no single
load bearing structure it would take an immense

00:01:51.340 --> 00:01:53.369
explosion to take down these towers.

00:01:53.369 --> 00:01:58.840
The soviets instead decided it best to circle
around the tower outside its effective range,

00:01:58.840 --> 00:02:00.670
and later negotiate a surrender.

00:02:00.670 --> 00:02:05.229
The Zoo Tower, was just the first of many
anti-aircraft towers built throughout major

00:02:05.229 --> 00:02:06.570
Nazi cities.

00:02:06.570 --> 00:02:11.040
Berlin had 3 pairs of towers forming a defensive
triangle around the city’s centre and central

00:02:11.040 --> 00:02:12.040
government.

00:02:12.040 --> 00:02:17.200
The first pair in Berlin Zoo, the next at
Friedrichshain and the third in Humboldthain.

00:02:17.200 --> 00:02:22.430
2 more pairs were constructed to protect the
vital port of Hamburg, and finally 3 more

00:02:22.430 --> 00:02:26.950
pairs were constructed in Vienna, Austria
to form another defensive triangle around

00:02:26.950 --> 00:02:28.270
the city’s center.

00:02:28.270 --> 00:02:33.120
Amazingly, many of these towers remain intact,
despite numerous efforts to destroy them.

00:02:33.120 --> 00:02:38.090
Stark reminders for the inhabitants of these
cities of an evil war waged by their ancestors.

00:02:38.090 --> 00:02:42.040
The towers were ordered to be built by Hitler
after the devastating and merciless bombing

00:02:42.040 --> 00:02:46.379
raids of civilian targets in Berlin in 1940
by the British RAF.

00:02:46.379 --> 00:02:50.990
Furious, Hitler demanded top priority for
the project, ordering railway and shipping

00:02:50.990 --> 00:02:57.710
traffic to be diverted to satisfy the 1600
tons of material needed for every day of construction.

00:02:57.710 --> 00:03:01.959
With the help of forced labour the Zoo tower
was completed in just 6 months and used a

00:03:01.959 --> 00:03:08.330
total of 78 thousand tons of gravel, 35 thousand
tons of cement, and 92 hundred tons of steel,

00:03:08.330 --> 00:03:12.190
along with the immense amount of wood required
for the moulds that formed the shape of the

00:03:12.190 --> 00:03:13.190
towers.

00:03:13.190 --> 00:03:16.880
To this day you can still see the outlines
of the planks that formed this moulds.

00:03:16.880 --> 00:03:19.180
Each tower complex consisted of a tower pair.

00:03:19.180 --> 00:03:23.849
An attack tower, named the G-Tower and a communications
tower called the L tower.

00:03:23.849 --> 00:03:28.350
All communications towers took this rectangular
shape, and came equipped with radio equipment,

00:03:28.350 --> 00:03:33.440
spotlights, their own anti-aircraft guns and
were tasked with coordinating the attack.

00:03:33.440 --> 00:03:38.120
The attack towers came in a variety of shapes,
with the earlier generation fortresses taking

00:03:38.120 --> 00:03:43.870
this square form 42 metres high and 57 metres
wide with turrets on each corner.

00:03:43.870 --> 00:03:48.220
Later in the war, as supplies became more
scarce, the designs took the form of these

00:03:48.220 --> 00:03:53.640
16-sided circular structures, 50.6 metres
high and 43 metres in diameter like this one

00:03:53.640 --> 00:03:55.870
in Augarten Park in Vienna.

00:03:55.870 --> 00:04:01.130
The attack towers were capable of firing 8000
rounds per minute, they formed a formidable

00:04:01.130 --> 00:04:05.739
air defence within their effective range,
but in all reality these towers had an extremely

00:04:05.739 --> 00:04:11.800
unimpressive track record with downing planes,
instead they acted primarily as a deterrent,

00:04:11.800 --> 00:04:16.519
any bomber that dared stray into their sights
would more than likely be shot down, but instead

00:04:16.519 --> 00:04:19.870
they mostly just kept well away from these
stationary positions.

00:04:19.870 --> 00:04:24.030
Each complex had an effective range of about
10 kilometres, so the complexes formed an

00:04:24.030 --> 00:04:28.210
important defence for the strategic positions
they covered, but left much of their cities

00:04:28.210 --> 00:04:29.210
unprotected.

00:04:29.210 --> 00:04:33.870
But these towers were more than just flak
towers, they served as valuable shelters for

00:04:33.870 --> 00:04:35.800
the civilian populations.

00:04:35.800 --> 00:04:39.400
Each tower had its own freshwater well and
vast stores of food.

00:04:39.400 --> 00:04:43.460
The towers had their own power generators,
with underground supply lines for fuel, and

00:04:43.460 --> 00:04:47.889
ofcourse massive stores of ammunition .The
towers even contained hospitals, with one

00:04:47.889 --> 00:04:52.710
tower containing a maternity ward where many
Berliners were born during air raids.

00:04:52.710 --> 00:04:56.560
Up to 15,000 civilians take refuge from the
relentless bombing.

00:04:56.560 --> 00:05:00.900
In the final days of the war up to 3 times
that number are reported to have crammed themselves

00:05:00.900 --> 00:05:02.690
into these concrete shells.

00:05:02.690 --> 00:05:06.370
Leading to horrifying conditions with people
dying in the cramped corridors with nowhere

00:05:06.370 --> 00:05:10.729
to bury them, toilets overflowing, and the
hospitals overflowing with injured and dead

00:05:10.729 --> 00:05:11.870
patients

00:05:11.870 --> 00:05:15.910
There are few visible signs of the devastation
these cities endured during the Second World

00:05:15.910 --> 00:05:20.060
War, thanks to the funding from the Marshall
Plan, cities were rebuilt brick-by-brick,

00:05:20.060 --> 00:05:22.030
removing the scars of the past.

00:05:22.030 --> 00:05:25.930
But these buildings remain, a testament to
the strength of their construction.

00:05:25.930 --> 00:05:29.289
All valuable materials have been stripped
from them, and multiple attempts have been

00:05:29.289 --> 00:05:33.520
made to destroy them, but with the proximity
of the surrounding city, and the thick concrete

00:05:33.520 --> 00:05:38.070
walls, the demolition has proven too costly
and difficult to undertake.

00:05:38.070 --> 00:05:41.430
Of the 6 towers in Berlin only one remains
mostly intact.

00:05:41.430 --> 00:05:46.669
The Zoo Tower was destroyed by British engineers,
but it didn’t go down without a fight.

00:05:46.669 --> 00:05:50.660
On the first attempt the engineers packed
it with 25 tonnes of dynamite, when the dust

00:05:50.660 --> 00:05:52.530
cleared the Tower remained standing.

00:05:52.530 --> 00:05:57.181
The tower finally collapsed on the third attempt
with 35 tonnes of dynamite, and from there

00:05:57.181 --> 00:06:01.729
the rubble was broken up and transported away,
and the land handed over to the Berlin Zoo

00:06:01.729 --> 00:06:04.240
where the hippopotamus park now resides.

00:06:04.240 --> 00:06:08.680
Both of Hamburgs attack towers remain, with
one being transformed into a nightclub and

00:06:08.680 --> 00:06:12.180
the other now acting as a giant energy storage
facility.

00:06:12.180 --> 00:06:16.729
The thick concrete walls provide fantastic
insulation, allowing the inside to be filled

00:06:16.729 --> 00:06:21.710
with 2 million litres of water which is heated
with biomethane and wood chip burners, solar

00:06:21.710 --> 00:06:25.110
panels on the roof, and waste heat from a
nearby factory.

00:06:25.110 --> 00:06:29.160
Providing heating for three thousand homes
and electricity for one thousand.

00:06:29.160 --> 00:06:33.699
All of Vienna’s towers remain, but only
one has been fully repurposed as an aquarium.

00:06:33.699 --> 00:06:37.830
Once again taking advantage of the thermal
stability of the interior for climate control

00:06:37.830 --> 00:06:42.419
of the tanks, while the walls of the aquarium
have been repurposed as a climbing wall.

00:06:42.419 --> 00:06:46.699
The attack tower of Arenbergpark has been
used as a storehouse for artwork too.

00:06:46.699 --> 00:06:50.900
This attack tower in Augarten park had one
attempted demolition, but it only managed

00:06:50.900 --> 00:06:54.539
to crack in the roof and collapse one of the
balconies,, and as a result the tower has

00:06:54.539 --> 00:06:58.620
been reinforced with steel wire to protect
the public from a possible collapse.

00:06:58.620 --> 00:07:03.289
Today these towers remain as a gargantuan
reminder of terrible war, but have been accepted

00:07:03.289 --> 00:07:05.569
and assimilated into the urban landscape.

00:07:05.569 --> 00:07:10.330
Parks have formed around them, zoos around
and inside them, and even have been repurposed

00:07:10.330 --> 00:07:12.830
to help towards a more sustainable future.

